A||||6||penetrator||Replacing Alu clutch pedal to Steel???||||5||
Z||000000||penetrator||02-08-2000||11:30 AM||||How hard is it to replace the Clutch Pedal? Do i have to empty the Clutch fluid?<BR>How long time does it take to replace it?<BR>Is there any things i must think of before i replace it?<P>Thanks<BR>Magnus||||reg||5
Z||000001||SpeedPhreak||02-08-2000||12:36 PM||||it was VERY easy...just follow the v8 archies directions.  it took me about 4 hours however i was watching a lot more football than working on my car at the time... if u are good at working on cars id estimate maybe 30-45 min if not....maybe 90.<P>the hardest part was pulling the clutch out...i broke a plastic sleave pretty good, but was still able to use it & the car worked fine.<P>good luck<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||penetrator||02-08-2000||12:40 PM||||Okey, then it will be about 120 minutes for me =)<P>Thanks<BR>Magnus||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||02-08-2000||01:08 PM||||One thing about it, the dia. of the pin on the new clutch pedal when I replaced mine was different from that on the old one. I measured the ID of the hole in the rod from the master cylinder with a caliper and measured the OD of the pin on the steel clutch (I think one was about 5/16 and the other about 3/8). Then I went up to ACE hardware and the guy had a box of bushings with flanges that were made out of bronze. He found one with the approximate dimensions and the diameters fit perfectly (little or no play). I had to cut its length down a little with a hacksaw and then file the end smooth. I used the springy washer that was on the original clutch pedal pin and put the spring clip in and it worked just fine.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 02-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||kidzwheelz||02-09-2000||12:07 PM||||  O.K. sign me up for clutch pedal replacement. After bleeding the system by V8 Archie's method three times, and still having some grinding going into gear, I finally inspected the pedal assembly. Needless to say, the new steel pedal is on order! Without this forum, I might still be bleeding the wonderfully designed little hydraulic clutch set-up! (Substitute your own list of expletives to replace that colorful little phrase!) ||||reg||1
Z||000005||kidzwheelz||02-12-2000||12:23 AM||||  Just finished installing my new steel pedal. It took about an hour with one trip to the parts store for the bushing mentioned before. Works like a champ, solved my grinding problem. Thanks, guys!||||reg||14
Z||000006||batboy||02-12-2000||08:50 AM||||When you replace the aluminum clutch pedal (84-85) with a steel one, the pivot pin is smaller (like what William was saying earlier). This means the banjo on the master cylinder rod is too big which allows some slop. If you don't want to mess with modifying something from the hardware store to make it fit, then order one of Rodney Dickman's little banjo bushings for $3 (U.S.) and you're completely back in business.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 02-12-2000).]||||reg||1
